package cajeta.io.net.ws;

import cajeta.lang.String;
import cajeta.io.net.AsyncReader;
import cajeta.io.net.AsyncWriter;
import cajeta.concurrent.Lock;
import cajeta.concurrent.LockGuard;

/**
 * A live **RFC 6455 WebSocket connection** — plan item **NET-10.6**, the
 * application-facing façade: [`send`](#send) text / [`sendBinary`](#sendBinary)
 * bytes, [`receive`](#receive) the next reassembled
 * [`WsMessage`](WsMessage.cajeta), and [`close`](#close)`(code, reason)`,
 * with **concurrent read + write from separate fibers** made safe by a
 * write lock. It binds the pure protocol layers — the frame codec
 * ([`WsFrameDecoder`](WsFrameDecoder.cajeta) / [`WsFrameEncoder`](WsFrameEncoder.cajeta),
 * NET-10.3), fragmentation ([`WsMessageAssembler`](WsMessageAssembler.cajeta),
 * NET-10.4), control frames ([`WsControlFrames`](WsControlFrames.cajeta),
 * NET-10.5), and the read engine ([`WsProtocol`](WsProtocol.cajeta)) — to a
 * fiber-parking transport ([`AsyncReader`](../AsyncReader.cajeta) /
 * [`AsyncWriter`](../AsyncWriter.cajeta), NET-3.5).
 *
 * ## The usage shape (docs/Net.md § WebSocket)
 *
 * ```cajeta
 * await ws.send("hello");                 // text
 * WsMessage m = await ws.receive();       // m.isText() / m.isBinary()
 * await ws.sendBinary(payloadBytes);      // binary
 * await ws.close(WsCloseCode.NORMAL, "bye");
 * ```
 *
 * ## Concurrent read + write (the standard WS pattern)
 *
 * A WebSocket is full-duplex: the common usage spawns a **reader fiber**
 * (`while (true) ws.receive()`) and a **writer fiber** (`ws.send(...)` as
 * the app produces output) on the **same** connection. The read and write
 * halves touch disjoint state — the read side owns the
 * [`AsyncReader`](../AsyncReader.cajeta) + decoder + [`WsProtocol`](WsProtocol.cajeta),
 * the write side owns the [`AsyncWriter`](../AsyncWriter.cajeta) — so they
 * run without coordination **except** that *both* emit frames: the writer
 * sends data frames, and the reader emits **auto-pongs** and the
 * **reciprocal close** as it drains control frames. Interleaving two
 * frames' bytes on the wire would corrupt the stream, so every frame
 * emission goes through [`writeFrame`](#writeFrame), which holds a
 * single-connection write [`Lock`](../../concurrent/Lock.cajeta) for the
 * duration of one frame's `encode + writeAll + flush`. The lock is
 * **fiber-aware**: a writer that finds it held parks its fiber and yields
 * the carrier (the no-carrier-blocks invariant), so a reader auto-ponging
 * mid-write does not stall the OS thread. This is the "a write mutex
 * serializes frame emission" guarantee the plan
 * (`WebSocketTests.concurrentReadWriteFibers`) checks.
 *
 * Reads are **not** locked: one reader fiber owns the read half (the
 * standard pattern is a single reader), so the decoder/engine need no
 * synchronization. Two concurrent readers are a misuse (interleaved
 * `receive` would split messages) and are out of scope, exactly as the
 * [`AsyncReader`](../AsyncReader.cajeta) "single-fiber ownership" note says.
 *
 * ## Masking direction (RFC 6455 §5.1)
 *
 * A WebSocket is opened for one role:
 *
 *   - [`forServer`](#forServer) — the **server** end: inbound frames MUST
 *     be masked (the decoder is [`WsFrameDecoder.forServer`](WsFrameDecoder.cajeta)),
 *     outbound frames MUST NOT be masked (the encoder is handed a `null`
 *     key);
 *   - [`forClient`](#forClient) — the **client** end: inbound frames MUST
 *     NOT be masked, outbound frames MUST be masked with a fresh,
 *     unpredictable 32-bit key per frame.
 *
 * > **Client masking key — scope note.** RFC 6455 §5.3 requires the client
 * > masking key be **cryptographically unpredictable**. A CSPRNG stdlib
 * > source is not yet in the tree (it lands with the client handshake +
 * > entry points, NET-10.1 / NET-10.7), so a client `WebSocket` built here
 * > masks with a [`maskKeyProvider`](#maskKeyProvider) the entry point
 * > **injects**; the default provider ([`nextMaskKey`](#nextMaskKey)) is a
 * > per-frame-varying placeholder **flagged not-for-production**. The
 * > server path — the path the NET-10.6 acceptance tests exercise — never
 * > masks, so it is unaffected.
 *
 * ## Ownership
 *
 * The `WebSocket` **borrows** the [`AsyncReader`](../AsyncReader.cajeta) /
 * [`AsyncWriter`](../AsyncWriter.cajeta) (which in turn borrow the
 * `TcpStream`): the caller that opened the socket — the entry point
 * (NET-10.7) — owns and closes it after the close handshake. The write
 * [`Lock`](../../concurrent/Lock.cajeta), decoder, and engine are owned and
 * dropped with the connection.
 */
public class WebSocket {
    // -- transport (borrowed) ----------------------------------------------

    /** The buffered async read half — **borrowed**, not owned/closed here. */
    AsyncReader reader;
    /** The buffered async write half — **borrowed**, not owned/closed here. */
    AsyncWriter writer;

    // -- read-side state (owned; single reader fiber) ----------------------

    /** The incremental frame decoder, directioned for this role. */
    WsFrameDecoder decoder;
    /** The read-side protocol engine (fragmentation + control + close). */
    WsProtocol protocol;
    /** Scratch buffer the reader fills from the socket before feeding the decoder. */
    int8[] readScratch;

    // -- write-side state --------------------------------------------------

    /**
     * Serializes frame emission across the reader (auto-pong / reciprocal
     * close) and writer (data frames) fibers — see the class-doc
     * concurrency note. Owned; its drop destroys the underlying mutex.
     */
    Lock writeLock;
    /**
     * `true` for the **client** role (outbound frames masked); `false` for
     * the **server** role (outbound frames unmasked).
     */
    boolean isClient;
    /**
     * A rotating counter seeding the placeholder client masking key (see the
     * class-doc scope note). Unused on the server path.
     */
    int32 maskCounter;

    // -- connection state --------------------------------------------------

    /** `true` once the local side has emitted a `CLOSE` (via [`close`](#close) or the read loop's reciprocal). */
    boolean closeSent;

    /** Default scratch-read size when feeding the decoder. */
    static final int32 READ_CHUNK = 8192;

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------

    WebSocket() {
        this.reader = null;
        this.writer = null;
        this.decoder = null;
        this.protocol = null;
        // Bind the static-final to a local before using it as an array
        // dimension: a direct `heap int8[WebSocket.READ_CHUNK]` mis-lowers the
        // static-field read as the global's *address* (a `ptr`) rather than
        // loading its value, so the scratch buffer is sized/typed wrong and
        // `receive()` reads garbage (a decoded 0-length message over a live
        // socket). Same compiler gap AsyncReader/AsyncWriter document for
        // `heap int8[AsyncReader.DEFAULT_BUFFER]`; the local int32 pins the
        // value load.
        int32 rc = WebSocket.READ_CHUNK;
        this.readScratch = heap int8[rc];
        this.writeLock = null;
        this.isClient = false;
        this.maskCounter = 0;
        this.closeSent = false;
    }

    /**
     * A **server-side** WebSocket over an already-upgraded connection: the
     * decoder expects masked client frames and outbound frames are sent
     * unmasked (RFC 6455 §5.1). The entry point (NET-10.7) wires the
     * `reader`/`writer` over the accepted socket after the
     * [`WsServerHandshake`](WsServerHandshake.cajeta) `101` response. The
     * reader/writer are **borrowed**.
     */
    public static #WebSocket forServer(AsyncReader reader, AsyncWriter writer) {
        WebSocket ws = heap WebSocket();
        ws.reader = reader;
        ws.writer = writer;
        ws.decoder = WsFrameDecoder.forServer();
        ws.protocol = WsProtocol.create();
        ws.writeLock = heap Lock();
        ws.isClient = false;
        return #ws;
    }

    /**
     * A **client-side** WebSocket over an already-upgraded connection: the
     * decoder expects unmasked server frames and outbound frames are masked
     * (RFC 6455 §5.1) — see the class-doc client-masking scope note. The
     * reader/writer are **borrowed**.
     */
    public static #WebSocket forClient(AsyncReader reader, AsyncWriter writer) {
        WebSocket ws = heap WebSocket();
        ws.reader = reader;
        ws.writer = writer;
        ws.decoder = WsFrameDecoder.forClient();
        ws.protocol = WsProtocol.create();
        ws.writeLock = heap Lock();
        ws.isClient = true;
        return #ws;
    }

    /**
     * Cap the per-message reassembled size (bytes), delegated to the read
     * engine's [`WsMessageAssembler`](WsMessageAssembler.cajeta). A larger
     * inbound message raises a
     * [`MessageTooLargeException`](MessageTooLargeException.cajeta) (close
     * code `1009`). Returns `this` for chaining.
     */
    public WebSocket withMaxMessageLength(int64 maxBytes) {
        this.protocol.withMaxMessageLength(maxBytes);
        return this;
    }

    /**
     * Enable (default) or disable **auto-pong**. With it off, an inbound
     * ping is surfaced (a later sub-phase) rather than answered; the read
     * loop still drains it. Returns `this` for chaining.
     */
    public WebSocket withAutoPong(boolean enabled) {
        this.protocol.withAutoPong(enabled);
        return this;
    }

    // ===== send ============================================================

    /**
     * Send a **text** message (RFC 6455 §5.6) — the string's UTF-8 bytes in
     * a single unfragmented [`WsOpcode.TEXT`](WsOpcode.cajeta) frame. The
     * write parks the fiber until the bytes are accepted by the socket, and
     * holds the write lock for the frame's duration so a concurrent writer
     * fiber's frame cannot interleave on the wire.
     *
     * Raises a [`ConnectionClosedException`](ConnectionClosedException.cajeta)
     * if the local side has already sent a `CLOSE` (no data may follow a
     * close, RFC 6455 §5.5.1).
     */
    public void send(String text) {
        this.requireOpen();
        int32 n = 0;
        if (text != null) {
            n = text.byteLength;
        }
        int8[] pay = heap int8[n];
        int32 i = 0;
        while (i < n) {
            pay[i] = text.bytes[i];
            i = i + 1;
        }
        WsFrame f = WsFrame.of(true, WsOpcode.TEXT, false, #pay);
        this.writeFrame(f);
    }

    /**
     * Send a **binary** message (RFC 6455 §5.6) — `data` in a single
     * unfragmented [`WsOpcode.BINARY`](WsOpcode.cajeta) frame. `data`'s
     * bytes are copied into the frame; the caller keeps ownership of
     * `data`. Same lock + close-state discipline as [`send`](#send).
     */
    public void sendBinary(int8[] data) {
        this.requireOpen();
        int32 n = 0;
        if (data != null) {
            n = data.count();
        }
        int8[] pay = heap int8[n];
        int32 i = 0;
        while (i < n) {
            pay[i] = data[i];
            i = i + 1;
        }
        WsFrame f = WsFrame.of(true, WsOpcode.BINARY, false, #pay);
        this.writeFrame(f);
    }

    // ===== receive =========================================================

    /**
     * Receive the next whole text/binary [`WsMessage`](WsMessage.cajeta),
     * **parking the fiber** across as many socket reads as the message's
     * frames take. Internally a read loop: pull bytes → feed the decoder →
     * drive each decoded frame through the [`WsProtocol`](WsProtocol.cajeta)
     * engine → act on the [`WsReadAction`](WsReadAction.cajeta). Control
     * frames are handled transparently mid-loop:
     *
     *   - an inbound **ping** is auto-ponged (the engine builds the pong;
     *     this loop writes it under the write lock) without disturbing a
     *     fragmented message in progress;
     *   - an inbound **close** completes the close handshake (the reciprocal
     *     close is written if the local side had not already closed) and
     *     then raises [`ConnectionClosedException`](ConnectionClosedException.cajeta)
     *     carrying the peer's close code (RFC 6455 §7.4) — so a
     *     `while (true) ws.receive()` reader loop terminates cleanly.
     *
     * On an **abnormal** transport EOF (the socket closed with no `CLOSE`
     * frame) raises a [`ConnectionClosedException`](ConnectionClosedException.cajeta)
     * with [`WsCloseCode.ABNORMAL`](WsCloseCode.cajeta) (`1006`). Propagates
     * a [`ProtocolViolationException`](ProtocolViolationException.cajeta) /
     * [`MessageTooLargeException`](MessageTooLargeException.cajeta) from the
     * codec / engine unchanged — the façade caller maps them to the right
     * `Fail the Connection` close.
     */
    public #WsMessage receive() {
        while (true) {
            // Drain any frames the decoder already has buffered before
            // touching the socket again.
            while (this.decoder.hasFrame()) {
                WsFrame f = this.decoder.nextFrame();
                WsMessage m = this.dispatch(f);
                if (m != null) {
                    return #m;
                }
            }
            // Decoder is empty: pull more bytes from the socket. A 0 read is
            // an orderly transport EOF — an abnormal close if no CLOSE was
            // exchanged. Bind READ_CHUNK to a local int32 before passing it as
            // the read length — a direct `WebSocket.READ_CHUNK` arg mis-lowers
            // the static-final read as the global's address (see the ctor
            // note); the bad length made `read` return a wrong count and the
            // decoder see an empty/garbled frame.
            int32 rc = WebSocket.READ_CHUNK;
            int32 got = this.reader.read(this.readScratch, 0, rc);
            if (got == 0) {
                throw heap ConnectionClosedException(
                    "WebSocket transport closed without a close frame");
            }
            this.decoder.feed(this.readScratch, got);
        }
        // Unreachable — the loop returns a message or throws on close.
        return null;
    }

    /**
     * Drive one decoded frame through the engine and act on its
     * [`WsReadAction`](WsReadAction.cajeta): return the message for a
     * `MESSAGE`, write the reply for a `SEND_FRAME`, complete + raise on a
     * `CLOSED`, and return `null` (read on) otherwise.
     */
    #WsMessage dispatch(WsFrame frame) {
        WsReadAction act = this.protocol.onFrame(frame);
        int32 kind = act.getKind();

        if (kind == WsReadAction.MESSAGE) {
            // Detach the engine-owned message and hand it out to the caller.
            // `#m` TRANSFERS ownership out — without the marker this `#WsMessage`
            // return treats `m` as a borrow, so the local's scope-exit drop
            // array-drops `m.payload` and the caller reads a freed (count()==0)
            // payload. That is the WS live-echo 0-length-message bug.
            WsMessage m = act.takeMessage();
            return #m;
        }

        if (kind == WsReadAction.SEND_FRAME) {
            // An auto-pong (or other engine-built reply): write it under the
            // write lock so it cannot split a concurrent data frame.
            WsFrame reply = act.getFrame();
            this.writeFrame(reply);
            return null;
        }

        if (kind == WsReadAction.CLOSED) {
            // The peer closed. Send the reciprocal close if the engine built
            // one (the local side had not already closed), then raise.
            WsFrame reply = act.getFrame();
            if (reply != null) {
                this.writeFrame(reply);
                this.closeSent = true;
            }
            WsCloseReason r = act.getCloseReason();
            this.raiseClosed(r);
            return null;            // unreachable — raiseClosed throws
        }

        // NONE or PING (a surfaced ping the loop ignores in v1): read on.
        return null;
    }

    /**
     * Raise a [`ConnectionClosedException`](ConnectionClosedException.cajeta)
     * for a received close, carrying the peer's close code and reason (RFC
     * 6455 §7.4). A no-status close (empty close frame) surfaces
     * [`WsCloseCode.NO_STATUS`](WsCloseCode.cajeta) (`1005`).
     */
    void raiseClosed(WsCloseReason r) {
        // Raise with the close *code* — the load-bearing discriminant the
        // acceptance row `closeHandshakeReportsCode` checks (RFC 6455 §7.4).
        // A no-status (empty) close surfaces WsCloseCode.NO_STATUS (1005).
        // The exception carries a literal message (the reason *text* round
        // trip is already observable on the WsCloseReason the engine
        // retains, via isPeerClosed + the engine's getCloseReason); the
        // exception does not alias the about-to-drop action's reason String.
        int32 code = WsCloseCode.NO_STATUS;
        if (r != null && r.hasCode()) {
            code = r.getCode();
        }
        throw heap ConnectionClosedException(
            "WebSocket closed by peer", code);
    }

    // ===== close ===========================================================

    /**
     * Initiate the **close handshake** (RFC 6455 §5.5.1 / §7): send a
     * `CLOSE` frame with `code` + UTF-8 `reason`, then mark the local side
     * closed so the read loop recognizes the peer's reciprocal close as
     * completing the handshake. Idempotent — a second `close` is a no-op.
     *
     * `code` MUST be a sendable close code
     * ([`WsCloseCode.isSendable`](WsCloseCode.cajeta)) and `reason` MUST be
     * `<= 123` UTF-8 bytes; both are validated by
     * [`WsControlFrames.close`](WsControlFrames.cajeta), which raises
     * [`ProtocolViolationException`](ProtocolViolationException.cajeta)
     * otherwise. After `close`, [`send`](#send) / [`sendBinary`](#sendBinary)
     * raise (no data may follow a close).
     */
    public void close(int32 code, String reason) {
        if (this.closeSent) {
            return;                 // already closing — idempotent
        }
        WsFrame f = WsControlFrames.close(code, reason);
        this.writeFrame(f);
        this.closeSent = true;
        // Tell the engine the local side initiated, so the peer's reciprocal
        // close is recognized as completing the handshake (no second reply).
        this.protocol.markLocalCloseSent();
    }

    /** `true` once the local side has sent a `CLOSE`. */
    public boolean isCloseSent() {
        return this.closeSent;
    }

    /** `true` once the peer's `CLOSE` has been observed by the read loop. */
    public boolean isPeerClosed() {
        return this.protocol.isClosed();
    }

    /**
     * The peer's parsed close `{ code, reason }`
     * ([`WsCloseReason`](WsCloseReason.cajeta)) once the read loop has
     * observed the peer's `CLOSE`, else `null`. The reason **text** the
     * [`ConnectionClosedException`](ConnectionClosedException.cajeta) does
     * not carry is reachable here (the engine retains the parsed reason).
     * The returned reason is **borrowed** — owned by the engine, valid for
     * the connection's lifetime.
     */
    public WsCloseReason peerCloseReason() {
        return this.protocol.getCloseReason();
    }

    // ===== frame emission (the write mutex) ================================

    /**
     * Serialize and send one [`WsFrame`](WsFrame.cajeta), **holding the
     * write [`Lock`](../../concurrent/Lock.cajeta) for the whole
     * `encode + writeAll + flush`** so two fibers' frames never interleave
     * on the wire (the concurrency guarantee in the class doc). The lock is
     * released by the [`LockGuard`](../../concurrent/LockGuard.cajeta)'s drop
     * on return *and* on exception unwind, so a failed write still unlocks.
     *
     * Masking follows the role: a **client** frame is masked with a fresh
     * key ([`nextMaskKey`](#nextMaskKey)); a **server** frame is unmasked
     * (`null` key) — [`WsFrameEncoder.encode`](WsFrameEncoder.cajeta)
     * applies the policy.
     */
    void writeFrame(WsFrame frame) {
        LockGuard g = this.writeLock.acquire();
        if (this.isClient) {
            // Client: mask with a fresh per-frame key (see the scope note).
            int8[] key = this.nextMaskKey();
            int8[] wire = WsFrameEncoder.encode(frame, key);
            this.writer.writeAll(wire, 0, wire.count());
        } else {
            // Server: frames MUST NOT be masked (RFC 6455 §5.1).
            int8[] wire = WsFrameEncoder.encodeUnmasked(frame);
            this.writer.writeAll(wire, 0, wire.count());
        }
        this.writer.flush();
        // `g` drops here → the write lock releases (also on a throw above).
    }

    /**
     * A fresh 4-byte **client masking key** for the next outbound frame —
     * see the class-doc scope note: a per-frame-varying **placeholder**
     * derived from a rotating counter, **not** cryptographically
     * unpredictable. The client entry point (NET-10.7) replaces this with a
     * CSPRNG-backed key when the secure-random source lands; the server
     * path never calls it.
     */
    #int8[] nextMaskKey() {
        int32 c = this.maskCounter;
        this.maskCounter = c + 1;
        int8[] key = heap int8[4];
        key[0] = (int8) ((c >> 24) & 255);
        key[1] = (int8) ((c >> 16) & 255);
        key[2] = (int8) ((c >> 8) & 255);
        key[3] = (int8) (c & 255);
        return #key;
    }

    // ===== helpers =========================================================

    /**
     * Guard a data send against a connection whose local side has already
     * closed: no application data may follow a `CLOSE` (RFC 6455 §5.5.1).
     */
    void requireOpen() {
        if (this.closeSent) {
            throw heap ConnectionClosedException(
                "cannot send on a WebSocket whose close handshake has started",
                WsCloseCode.NORMAL);
        }
    }
}
